But there are 5 important problems
1. Your client is still determining the actual result
This is the biggest architectural issue.
In app.ts:
const hashHex = await sha256Hex(`${resolveData.blockhash}:${state.roundId}`);
const seed = parseInt(hashHex.slice(0, 8), 16) >>> 0;

generateBoardFromSeed(seed);

Then:
if (poisonSet.has(index)) {
    declareLoss(level);
}

The browser is therefore deciding:
"This cell is poison."

The server has the same logic, but the client is still the authority for the displayed game result.
For a real-money/token game, you want:
CLIENT
  ↓
"I choose cell 3"
  ↓
SERVER
  ↓
Solana blockhash
  ↓
fairness.ts
  ↓
WIN / LOSS
  ↓
CLIENT displays result

Not:
CLIENT
  ↓
calculate poison
  ↓
decide WIN/LOSS

2. Your "verification" isn't fully independent
Your current verification does this:
const record = rounds.get(String(body.roundId || ""));

Then:
connection.getBlock(record.targetSlot)

So the verifier depends on the server's stored:
roundId
targetSlot
blockhash

That's okay for a server verification endpoint, but it isn't really an independent public verifier.
A proper public verifier should be able to take:
Round ID
Solana slot
Player choice

and independently calculate:
blockhash
      ↓
SHA256(blockhash:roundId)
      ↓
poison cells
      ↓
WIN/LOSS

Your fairness.ts is already structured nicely for this.
3. Your round only gets randomness once — this part is actually good
I initially expected your code to generate a new Solana block for every level.
But because of:
if (state.randomnessResolved) {
    return;
}

you only call /resolve once.
So the actual flow is:
Level 1 choice
     ↓
Solana block
     ↓
ONE seed
     ↓
Generate all 10 levels
     ↓
Level 2 uses same board
     ↓
Level 3 uses same board
     ↓
...
     ↓
Level 10

That's exactly what you want if the intention is one random board per round.
Good.
4. ROUND_OFFSET = 3 is your main fairness/security weakness
You currently do:
const currentSlot = await connection.getSlot("confirmed");

targetSlot: currentSlot + ROUND_OFFSET

with:
ROUND_OFFSET = 3;

So:
Player clicks
      ↓
Server receives request
      ↓
Server picks current + 3
      ↓
wait
      ↓
blockhash

The problem isn't that +3 is mathematically wrong.
The important issue is that your server chooses the future slot after receiving the player's choice.
For a serious wagering system, you want the randomness commitment/selection mechanism to be much harder for any party to influence.
For your current POC, it's acceptable.
For production money:
don't treat this blockhash approach as sufficient provable randomness.
Use a proper VRF/oracle or an on-chain randomness mechanism.
5. Your payout is entirely client-side
This is a much bigger issue if you're actually going to stake your token.
You currently do:
state.finalPayout =
    getCurrentPayout();

and cashout:
function handleCashOut() {
    ...
    state.finalPayout = payout;
    state.status = "cashed_out";
}

That means:
Cash Out
   ↓
browser changes state
   ↓
browser says payout = X

There is no actual settlement.
For a token casino, the eventual architecture needs:
Player wallet
     ↓
stake transaction
     ↓
game program
     ↓
round locked
     ↓
randomness resolved
     ↓
WIN
     ↓
cashout transaction
     ↓
program verifies state
     ↓
token payout

The browser should never be trusted to say:
"Pay me 349.68x."

Your fairness.ts is actually the cleanest part
I would keep this general structure:
blockhash + roundId
        ↓
SHA256
        ↓
hashHex
        ↓
seed
        ↓
Fisher-Yates
        ↓
poison cells

And:
export function outcomeForChoice(
  poisonCells: Set<number>,
  choice: number
): Outcome {
  return poisonCells.has(choice - 1)
    ? "LOSS"
    : "WIN";
}

The 1–5 public choice → 0–4 internal index conversion is consistent.
That's good.
What I would change
Your architecture should become:
                 ┌──────────────────┐
                 │     CLIENT       │
                 │                  │
                 │ Choose cell 1-5  │
                 └────────┬─────────┘
                          │
                          ▼
                 ┌──────────────────┐
                 │     SERVER       │
                 │                  │
                 │ Lock round       │
                 │ Record choice    │
                 └────────┬─────────┘
                          │
                          ▼
                 ┌──────────────────┐
                 │ SOLANA           │
                 │                  │
                 │ Future block     │
                 │ blockhash        │
                 └────────┬─────────┘
                          │
                          ▼
                 ┌──────────────────┐
                 │ fairness.ts      │
                 │                  │
                 │ SHA256           │
                 │ seed             │
                 │ shuffle          │
                 │ poison cells     │
                 └────────┬─────────┘
                          │
                          ▼
                    WIN / LOSS
                          │
                          ▼
                 ┌──────────────────┐
                 │     CLIENT       │
                 │                  │
                 │ Show animation   │
                 └──────────────────┘

The important change is:
server determines the result; client renders it.
One more important thing: your current server doesn't actually enforce the game
For example, /api/rounds/resolve returns:
{
  roundId,
  targetSlot,
  blockhash
}

It doesn't return:
WIN
LOSS

The client then calculates that itself.
I'd change that.
Something like:
{
  roundId,
  level,
  choice,
  targetSlot,
  blockhash,
  outcome,
  poisonCells
}

Then the client simply does:
response.outcome === "WIN"

and renders the appropriate animation.
You can optionally not expose all poison cells immediately if you want the UI to reveal only the selected cell.
Recommended division
fairness.ts
Only deterministic fairness logic:
blockhash
roundId
↓
hash
↓
seed
↓
poison positions
↓
outcome

server.ts
Game authority:
round creation
choice locking
Solana slot
blockhash
fairness calculation
WIN/LOSS
round state
cashout authorization

app.ts
UI only:
buttons
animations
board
wallet
messages
display payout
fairness UI

Eventually Solana program
Money authority:
stake
escrow
round state
randomness proof
win/loss
cashout
token transfer

Bottom line
Your fairness algorithm itself is coherent. The biggest thing to fix isn't fairness.ts; it's the trust boundary.
Currently:
Client calculates result
Server can reproduce result

You want:
Server calculates result
Client displays result

And when real token staking is introduced:
On-chain program controls money
Server coordinates the game
Client only presents it

That's the direction I'd take before adding more UI or animations.


